Athenian democracy (around 500-322 BCE) was an experiment because it was unprecedented — citizens directly participated in lawmaking and judging, rather than being ruled by a king or oligarchy.

STRENGTHS:
 - direct participation gave citizens real political power;
 - public deliberation in the Assembly developed the art of oratory and rational debate;
 - accountability — magistrates could be voted out, sued, or even ostracised;
 - it inspired later democratic experiments worldwide.

LIMITATIONS:
 - CITIZENSHIP was restricted to free adult MALES of Athenian parentage; women, slaves and resident foreigners were excluded;
 - SLAVERY was central to Athenian economy — slave labour freed citizens for politics;
 - DEMAGOGUES (skilled orators) could manipulate the Assembly;
 - it was suited only to a small city-state — not scalable to a large empire.
